To inherit functions from other classes in Python, you can use the `class` keyword followed by the name of the parent class in parentheses. The subclass can then inherit all the attributes and methods of the parent class.

Here is an example:
```python
class ParentClass:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def print_value(self):
        print(self.value)

class SubClass(ParentClass):
    def __init__(self, value, name):
        super().__init__(value)
        self.name = name

    def print_name(self):
        print(self.name)

sub = SubClass(10, "NAME_1")
sub.print_value() # Output: 10
sub.print_name() # Output: NAME_1
```
In this example, the `SubClass` inherits the `print_value` function from the `ParentClass` by using the `super().__init__(value)` in the `__init__` method. The `SubClass` also has its own `print_name` function. When we call the `print_value` and `print_name` methods on an instance of the `SubClass`, both methods are called and their respective outputs are displayed.

