def binary_search(arr, x):
    if len(arr) == 0:
        return -1
    if len(arr) == 1:
        return arr[0] == x
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    if arr[mid] == x:
        return mid
    if arr[mid] < x:
        return binary_search(arr[:mid], x)
    return binary_search(arr[mid+1:], x)
```

The function binary_search takes two arguments: arr, which is an array of values, and x, which is the target value to be searched for.

The function first checks if the length of the array is 0. If so, it returns -1, indicating that the target value is not found in the array.

If the length of the array is not 0, the function checks if the length of the array is 1. If so, it compares the single element in the array with the target value x. If they are equal, it returns the index of the element. Otherwise, it returns -1.

If the length of the array is not 0 or 1, the function calculates the midpoint of the array and uses it as the current search point.

The function then checks if the element at the midpoint is equal to the target value x. If so, it returns the index of the element.

If the element at the midpoint is not equal to x, the function uses conditional statements to determine whether the target value is in the left half of the array or the right half of the array.

If the target value is in the left half of the array, the function calls the binary_search function recursively on the left half of the array.

If the target value is in the right half of the array, the function calls the binary_search function recursively on the right half of the array.

